 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Vika has had a terrible day, and she takes it out on Max. She tells him exactly what she thinks, and Max replies with some thoughts about walls. First, the Russian.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вик, что с тобой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "В чём дело, Макс?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "В чем дело?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Меня сегодня чуть не уволили с работы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "И все благодаря тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Макс, ты рушишь мою жизнь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Все.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Не хочу тебя видеть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Хочешь, я тебе скажу, в чем дело?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты постоянно все усложняешь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А виноватыми делаешь других.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Может, стоит поискать причины в себе?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Меня всегда удивляло...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "почему люди сами возводят между собой границы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Но есть границы, которые мы специально возводим...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "чтобы с удовольствием их разрушить.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "The last scene of the episode, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вик, что с тобой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Vik, what is wrong?' Let us focus on the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вик",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a short form of Vika. Friends drop the last vowel when they call someone. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "что",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "с",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "тобой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'what with you', which is the way Russians ask what is wrong.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "В чём дело, Макс?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is the matter, Max?' Literally, 'in what the matter', and it is a useful phrase. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "дело",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " on its own means 'business' or 'affair'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "В чем дело?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is the matter?' It is the same question again, now without his name. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "В",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "чем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'in what', the form that 'what' takes after 'in'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Меня сегодня чуть не уволили с работы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I almost got fired today.' Russian says 'me they fired', using ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "уволили",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", so you do not say who. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "чуть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'nearly', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "с",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "работы",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'from work'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "И все благодаря тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And it is all thanks to you.' Let us break down the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "благодаря",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "тебе",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'thanks to you'. It is usually said in a good sense, but Vika says it bitterly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Макс, ты рушишь мою жизнь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Max, you are ruining my life.' Now we can consider the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "рушишь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'you knock down', used for walls and, as here, for lives. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "мою",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "жизнь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'my life', and only the first word changes its ending.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Все.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That is it.' One word, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Все",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which Russians say as a verdict. It closes the matter.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Не хочу тебя видеть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I do not want to see you.' It is just the plain 'I do not want' and 'to see you', with nothing to soften it. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "тебя",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the form of 'you' after ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "видеть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", to see.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Хочешь, я тебе скажу, в чем дело?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Want me to tell you what is going on?' Now let's focus on the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Хочешь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "я",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "тебе",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "скажу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " word-for-word means 'want, I will tell you'. Russian leaves out 'that'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты постоянно все усложняешь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You make everything so complicated.' We can break down the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "усложняешь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from a word meaning complicated. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "постоянно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'constantly'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А виноватыми делаешь других.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then you blame everyone else.' Now we can break down the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "виноватыми",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
